# Per-host terminal colours for the `ssh` wrapper in shell/ssh-theme.sh.
# Copy to ~/.ssh/themes (or point $SSH_THEMES_FILE at it) and edit.
#
#   <pattern>   <background>   [foreground]
#
# <pattern> is a glob, matched first against the host as typed, then against the
# hostname ssh resolves it to. First match wins. Colours are a flavour name --
# Catppuccin (latte, frappe, macchiato, mocha), Gruvbox dark (gruvbox,
# gruvbox-hard, gruvbox-soft), foxpuccin, the warm pastel palette this
# machine's tmux uses, or manjuu, the iTerm profile's own colours -- or six
# hex digits with NO leading '#', since '#' starts a comment. A host with no line here is left alone: the terminal
# keeps whatever background it already has.

my-server            mocha
my-laptop            macchiato
build-box            gruvbox     # match whatever its tmux sits on

# *.prod.example.com   ff5555  ffffff    # shout about production

# Restore colour. Only needed if your terminal ignores OSC 111/110 ("reset to
# the profile's colours"), which is what the wrapper uses by default.
# default   121212

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The other direction: a machine can declare its OWN colour. On that machine put
# a single line in ~/.ssh/host-theme (or /etc/ssh/host-theme for every user):
#
#   gruvbox
#
# Same colour syntax as above, minus the host pattern. That colour goes on the
# window from both sides:
#
#   - every client that connects gets it, even one with none of this installed.
#     A client with its own line here overrides it: it sends LC_SSH_THEME=client
#     and the host stays quiet, so list a self-declaring host above only when
#     you want to override it.
#
#   - terminals sitting at the machine itself get it too, so `ssh that-box` from
#     here and a window opened over there land on the same background. Keep the
#     line above and the host's own file on the same flavour NAME and they
#     cannot drift apart -- both resolve through one table in ssh-theme.sh.
#     Set DOTFILES_NO_LOCAL_THEME=1 on the machine to keep its local windows
#     alone and only announce to clients.
